[{"text":"Overview.","anchor":"see-also","section":"See also"},{"text":"The internal representation (FeynCalcIntenral or FCI) is how FeynCalc internally “sees” the objects. For example, a \\(4\\)-dimensional 4-vector is represented by","anchor":"internal-representation","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]], Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^{\\mu }\\]","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Pair is one of the most basic FeynCalc objects. Depending on its arguments, it can represent a \\(4\\)-vector, a metric tensor","anchor":"cb1-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]], LorentzIndex[\\[Nu]]]","anchor":"cb2","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{g}^{\\mu \\nu }\\]","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"or a scalar product of two 4-vectors","anchor":"cb2-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Pair[Momentum[p], Momentum[q]]","anchor":"cb3","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}\\cdot \\overline{q}\\]","anchor":"cb3-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Another essential object is DiracGamma that is used to represent Dirac matrices. An uncontracted Dirac matrix is","anchor":"cb3-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"DiracGamma[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]]]","anchor":"cb4","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }^{\\mu }\\]","anchor":"cb4-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"and for a Feynman slash we use","anchor":"cb4-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"DiracGamma[Momentum[p]]","anchor":"cb5","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\gamma }\\cdot \\overline{p}\\]","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"The Levi-Civita-Tensor is","anchor":"cb5-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Eps[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]], LorentzIndex[\\[Nu]], LorentzIndex[\\[Rho]], LorentzIndex[\\[Sigma]]]","anchor":"cb6","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu \\nu \\rho \\sigma }\\]","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"or, when contracted with 4-momenta","anchor":"cb6-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"Eps[Momentum[p1], Momentum[p2], Momentum[q1], Momentum[q2]]","anchor":"cb7","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\overline{\\text{p1}}\\;\\overline{\\text{p2}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q1}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q2}}}\\]","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"This notation (momenta in the index slots) is also used in many other tools (e.g. FORM). The advantage is, that we do not need to canonicalize the indices of the Levi-Civita-Tensor, e.g. to ensure that","anchor":"cb7-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"diff = Eps[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]], Momentum[p2], Momentum[q1], Momentum[q2]] Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Mu]], Momentum[p1]] - Eps[LorentzIndex[\\[Nu]], Momentum[p2], Momentum[q1], Momentum[q2]] Pair[LorentzIndex[\\[Nu]], Momentum[p1]]","anchor":"cb8","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{\\text{p1}}^{\\mu } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\mu \\overline{\\text{p2}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q1}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q2}}}-\\overline{\\text{p1}}^{\\nu } \\bar{\\epsilon }^{\\nu \\overline{\\text{p2}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q1}}\\;\\overline{\\text{q2}}}\\]","anchor":"cb8-2","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"diff // Contract","anchor":"cb9","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"\\[0\\]","anchor":"cb9-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"is zero.","anchor":"cb9-1","section":"Internal representation"},{"text":"The internal representation is useful for the internal programming FeynCalc, but obviously too cumbersome for the user input. This is why FeynCalc also has an external representation (FeynCalcExternal or FCE), that is concise and convenient.","anchor":"external-representation","section":"External representation"},{"text":"Let us start with the \\(4\\)-vector. In the FCE-notation it is just FV (“FourVector”)","anchor":"external-representation","section":"External representation"},{"text":"FV[p, \\[Mu]]","anchor":"cb10","section":"External representation"},{"text":"\\[\\overline{p}^{\\mu }\\]","anchor":"cb10-1","section":"External representation"}]